yed as the abstract.\n\nImage: 901. The structure of viridogrisein (etamycin)\n\nYou have access to this article\n\n## Article information\n\nDOI\n    https://doi.org/10.1039/JR9580004466\n\nArticle type\n    Paper\n\n### Download Citation\n\nJ. Chem. Soc., 1958, 4466-4470\n\n\n### 901. The structure of viridogrisein (etamycin)\n\nR. B. Arnold, A. W. Johnson and A. B. Mauger, J. Chem. Soc., 1958, 4466 DOI: 10.1039/JR9580004466\n\nTo request permission to reproduce material from this article, please go to the Copyright Clearance Center request page.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n575\nVOL.\nXXXII\nNO. \n6\nTHE\nJOURNAL \nOF\nANTIBIOTICS (https://electronicsandbooks.com/edt/manual/Magazine/J/Journal%20of%20Antibiotics/1979%20Vol.%2032/32_575.pdf)\nciteturn10search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Structure ... Neoviridogrisein ... U.S.A. and etamycin by Bristol Laboratories, Inc., Syracuse, New York, U.S.A. ... SEINo, Kaken Chemical Co., Tokyo, Japan.\n577\nVOL.\nXXXII \nNO. \n6\nTHE\nJOURNAL \nOF\nANTIBIOTICS\nFig. 3.\nStructure \nof mikamycin B group (sub-group\nB II)\nantibiotic.\nFig. 4.\nStructures \nof\nneoviri\ndogriseins \nI, II, III,\nand IV.\nR \nX \nNeoviridogrisein \nI \n- CH2CH3 \n -H \nViridogrisein \n(= Etomycin)\nNeoviridogrisein \nII \n -CH3 \n -H \nNeoviridogrisein \nIII \n-CH2CH3 \n-OH \nNeoviridogrisein \nIV \n - CH3\n-OH\n(= Viridogrisein)\nMaterials \nand Methods\n    1. Materials \n   (1) Antibiotics and reagents \n   \nViridogrisein and griseoviridin were kindly supplied by Parke, Davis & Co., Detroit 32, Mich., \nU.S.A. and etamycin by Bristol Laboratories, Inc., Syracuse, New York, U.S.A. Pre-coated silica gel \nplates (Pre-Coated TLC Plates Silica Gel 60 F-254) and silica gel for column chromatography \n(Kieselgel 60 Art. 7734) were obtained from E. Merck, Darmstadt, Germany; pre-coated cellulose \nsheets (Eastman Chromagram \nSheet No. 6065) from Eastman Kodak Co., Rochester, New York \n14650, U.S.A.; and Sephadex LH-20 from Pharmacia Fine Chemicals AB, Uppsala, Sweden. Other \nmaterials were purchased from commercial sources. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBiosynthesis and Synthesis of Griseoviridin and Viridogrisein (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13588673/)\nciteturn10search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Griseoviridin (GV) and viridogrisein (VG, also known as etamycin) are a pair of structurally distinct but functionally synergistic antibiotics produced by Streptomyces griseoviridis that were first isolated in Texas, USA in the 1950s.^{[ref-type: bibr] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 1]],[ref-type: bibr] 2 [Source reference: [Source link 2]]} These compounds belong to the streptogramin family of antibiotics, which are characterized by the cooperative action of the type A and B members in inhibiting Gram-positive bacterial growth.^{[ref-type: bibr] 3 [Source reference: [Source link 3]]} Griseoviridin belongs to type A streptogramins, which consist of cyclic polyunsaturated macrolactones derived from a hybrid peptide/polyketide pathway.^{[ref-type: bibr] 4 [Source reference: [Source link 4]]} Structural variation in type A streptogramins can arise from desaturation of the Pro residue and from its substitution with Ala or Cys.^{[ref-type: bibr] 5 [Source reference: [Source link 5]]} In addition to griseoviridin, type A streptogramins also include pristinamycin II, madumycin II, and the semisynthetic derivative dalfopristin ([ref-type: fig] Figure 1 [Source reference: [Source link 6]]).\n\n# Biosynthesis and Synthesis of Griseoviridin and Viridogrisein\n\nGriseoviridin and viridogrisein are synergistic type A/B streptogramin antibiotics that cooperatively inhibit protein translation in Gram–positive pathogens. This review provides an overview of their discovery and bioactivity and highlights key biosynthetic and synthetic advances that have enabled analog development to address antibiotic resistance. The review is organized into five sections:\n\n  1. Introduction\n  2. Structural assignment and biological studies\n  3. Biosynthesis\n  4. Synthesis\n  5. Conclusion\n\nGriseoviridin (GV) and viridogrisein (VG, also known as etamycin) are a pair of structurally distinct but functionally synergistic antibiotics produced by Streptomyces griseoviridis that were first isolated in Texas, USA in the 1950s.^{[ref-type: bibr] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 1]],[ref-type: bibr] 2 [Source reference: [Source link 2]]} These compounds belong to the streptogramin family of antibiotics, which are characterized by the cooperative action of the type A and B members in inhibiting Gram-positive bacterial growth.^{[ref-type: bibr] 3 [Source reference: [Source link 3]]} Griseoviridin belongs to type A streptogramins, which consist of cyclic polyunsaturated macrolactones derived from a hybrid peptide/polyketide pathway.^{[ref-type: bibr] 4 [Source reference: [Source link 4]]} Structural variation in type A streptogramins can arise from desaturation of the Pro residue and from its substitution with Ala or Cys.^{[ref-type: bibr] 5 [Source reference: [Source link 5]]} In addition to griseoviridin, type A streptogramins also include pristinamycin II, madumycin II, and the semisynthetic derivative dalfopristin ([ref-type: fig] Figure 1 [Source reference: [Source link 6]]). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNo Title (https://patentimages.storage.googleapis.com/53/35/70/8cdd11c74ebef4/KR100420238B1.pdf)\nciteturn10search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Chemical structure diagrams of virginiamycin-related compounds, arranged in three groups. ... **Bottom left structure: Etamycin A (neoviridogrisein IV, viridogrisein)** ... The diagrams depict peptide-like antibiotic chemical structures with labeled variable substituent positions (R, R₁, R₂, R₃, R₄), aromatic rings, heterocyclic rings, and amide linkages.\n10-0420238\n\n## Chemical structures and compound variants\n\n### Virginiamycin and related compounds\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nChemical structure diagrams of virginiamycin-related compounds, arranged in three groups.\n\n**Top left structure:**\n- R₁ = Me, virginiamycin S₁\n- R₁ = H, VS₄\n\n**Top right structure:**\n- R₁ = Me, R₂ = H, R₃ = —OH, R₄ = H — VS₂\n- R₁ = Me, R₂ = OH, R₃ = O, R₄ = Me — VS₃\n\n**Middle row structures (left to right):**\n- Patricin A\n- Patricin B\n- Vernamycin C, Noricin\n\n**Bottom left structure: Etamycin A (neoviridogrisein IV, viridogrisein)**\n- R₁ = OH (cis), R₂ = Me\n- Neoviridogrisein I: R₁ = H, R₂ = Et, R₃ = Me\n- Neoviridogrisein II: R₁ = H, R₂ = Me, R₃ = Me\n- Neoviridogrisein III: R₁ = OH, R₂ = Et, R₃ = Me\n- Cl-c: R₁ = Cl (cis), R₂ = Me, R₃ = Me\n- Cl-t: R₁ = Cl (trans), R₂ = Me, R₃ = Me\n- Viridogrisein II: R₁ = OH, R₂ = Et, R₃ = H\n\n**Bottom right structures:**\n- Grivamycin I, II: R₁ = OH (cis / trans), R₂ = Me\n- Grivamycin III: R₁ = OH, R₂ = H\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProQuest Dissertations (https://dalspaceb.library.dal.ca/server/api/core/bitstreams/95902b93-c154-4fbe-a733-29f5518fdd14/content)\nciteturn10search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.0 years ago; Structure ... coworkers (1957, 1958) showed etamycin and viridogrisein to be\n9 \nA. Structure \nThe antibiotic was discovered independently by three \nresearch groups and given three different names. Heinemann and \nco-workers (1955) isolated an antibiotic from cultures of a \nsoil isolate, Streptomyces \nlavendulae, \nand nam^.d it etamycin. \nAt about the same time, Bartz et al. \n(1955) isolated two \nantibiotics from culture filtrates of both an unidentified \nStreptomyces (P-D 04955) and a strain of Streptomyces \ngriseus \n(P-D 04797; Ehrlich et al., \n1955). The former was identified \nby Anderson et al. \n(1956) as Streptomyces \ngriseoviridus. \nOf \nthe two antibiotics, one proved to be a type-A streptogramin \nand was named griseoviridin (Vazquez, 1967). The other was a \ntype-B streptogramin, and was named viridogrisein. Sheehan and \ncoworkers (1957, 1958) showed etamycin and viridogrisein to be \nidentical. From Actinomyces \ndaghestanicus, \nToropova et \nal. \n(1959) later isolated antibiotic #6613, also identified as \netamycin (Krugylark et al., \n1961). \nEtamycin is hydrolysed by alkali at the ester bond between \nthreonine and phenylsarcosine (see Fig. IB) to generate a \nlinear polypeptide which, by acid hydrolysis, gives in \nequimolecular yield eight amino acids: 3-hydroxypicolinic \nacid, \nL-threonine, \nD-leucine, \nallo-hydroxy-D-proline, \nsarcosine, N, (5-dimethyl-L-leucine, L-alanine, and L-a-\nphenylsarcosine. L-a-phenylsarcosine \nand N, (5-dimethyl-L-\nleucine were not known in nature before their discovery in \netamycin (Heinemann et al., \n1955, Haskell et al., \n1955;--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlossary 541 (https://uodiyala.edu.iq/uploads/PDF%20ELIBRARY%20UODIYALA/EL27/6%20uodiyala%20Peptides%20Chemistry%20and%20Biology.pdf)\nciteturn10search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.6 years ago; Viomycin, viocin, vinactin A, florimycin, tuberactinomycin B, a cyclic 5-peptide (Formula, see p. 542) antibiotic from the culture broth of various Streptomyces sp. such as S. puniceus, S. floridae and S. vinaceus. ... Viridogrisein, → etamycin. ... Chemical structure labeled “Viomycins” with two variants: “R = OH Viomycin” and “R = H Tuberactinomycin B”.\nJ. Hruby, D. Patel in: Peptides: Synthesis, Structures, and Applications, E. Gutte (Ed.), p. 261, Academic Press, San Diego, 1995; M. Birnbaumer, Trends Endocrinol. Metab. 2000, 10, 406].\n\nViomycin, viocin, vinactin A, florimycin, tuberactinomycin B, a cyclic 5-peptide (Formula, see p. 542) antibiotic from the culture broth of various Streptomyces sp. such as S. puniceus, S. floridae and S. vinaceus. Vi