 depsipeptide counterpart to GV also referred to as etamycin (Fig. [ref-type: fig] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 1]]) [[ref-type: bibr] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 2]], [ref-type: bibr] 2 [Source reference: [Source link 3]]]. ... Chemical structures of griseoviridin (GV) and viridogrisein (VG)\n  * Streptomyces griseoviridis\n\nFunder: Institution: National Science Foundation of China Award: 81425022 Award: U1501223 Award: 41406195\n\nAward recipient: [name-style: western] Xie Yunchang [name-style: western] Ju Jianhua\n\nFunder: Institution: Chinese Academy of Sciences Award: XDA11030403\n\nAward recipient: [name-style: western] Ju Jianhua\n\nFunder: Institution: Natural Science Foundation of Guangdong Province Award: 2016A030312014\n\nAward recipient: [name-style: western] Ju Jianhua\n\n## Background\n\nThe actinomycete Streptomyces griseoviridis NRRL 2427 generates two types of unrelated streptogramins; these include griseoviridin (GV) belonging to the A-type cyclic polyunsaturated macrolactone subclass, and viridogrisein (VG), a B-type cyclic depsipeptide counterpart to GV also referred to as etamycin (Fig. [ref-type: fig] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 1]]) [[ref-type: bibr] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 2]], [ref-type: bibr] 2 [Source reference: [Source link 3]]]. GV and VG bind to the A and P sites, respectively, of the 50S bacterial ribosomal subunit thereby averting peptide bond formation during the elongation phase of protein translation. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIdentification of the biosynthetic gene cluster and regulatory cascade for the synergistic antibacterial antibiotics griseoviridin and viridogrisein in Streptomyces griseoviridis (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23161816/)\nciteturn10search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Identification of the biosynthetic gene cluster and regulatory cascade for the synergistic antibacterial antibiotics 